  <h1>India’s Women’s Reservation Bill 2023 — Towards Political Empowerment</h1>
  <div><em>The Hindu | Date: 2024 (post-enactment debates) • ~600 words</em></div>
  <h3>Introduction</h3><p>The Women’s Reservation Bill, 2023, officially the Constitution (106th Amendment) Act, was passed by Parliament to reserve 33% seats for women in the Lok Sabha and State Legislative Assemblies. The Hindu in 2024 analyzed the Bill’s implications, describing it as a historic milestone in gender justice. However, the timeline for implementation — tied to delimitation after the 2026 Census — raised concerns about delays.
________________________________________
Historical Background
•	First introduced in 1996, lapsed multiple times.
•	Rajya Sabha passed it in 2010, but Lok Sabha never voted.
•	Finally enacted in September 2023, marking a 27-year journey.
________________________________________</p>
  <h3>Body</h3><div>________________________________________<br/>Introduction<br/><br/>Key Provisions<br/>1.	33% Reservation: For women in Lok Sabha and State Assemblies.<br/>2.	Rotation System: Reserved constituencies will rotate after each delimitation.<br/>3.	Implementation Timeline: Operational only after delimitation based on Census (expected ~2029 elections).<br/>________________________________________<br/>Significance<br/>•	Political Empowerment: Increases women’s representation (currently ~15% in Lok Sabha).<br/>•	Democratic Deepening: Broader voices in policymaking.<br/>•	Gender Justice: Corrects historical exclusion from political power.<br/>•	Policy Outcomes: Global studies show women legislators prioritize health, education, and welfare.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Criticisms<br/>1.	Delayed Implementation: Linking to delimitation means no immediate benefit.<br/>2.	No Sub-Quota: Demands for OBC, minority sub-quotas within 33% not addressed.<br/>3.	Rotation Concerns: Frequent constituency rotation may hinder long-term political careers.<br/>4.	Tokenism Risk: Without addressing patriarchal party structures, impact may be limited.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Global Comparisons<br/>•	Rwanda: >60% women in Parliament with quotas.<br/>•	Nepal: 33% reservation in national and local legislatures.<br/>•	Nordic Nations: High representation achieved through party-level quotas.<br/>India lags far behind global peers, ranking ~140th in IPU’s women representation index (2023).<br/>________________________________________<br/>UPSC Relevance<br/>•	GS-II (Polity & Governance): Constitutional amendments, gender justice.<br/>•	GS-I (Society): Role of women in politics.<br/>•	Essay:<br/>o	“Empowering women empowers democracy.”<br/>o	“Reservation as a tool of social justice: promises and limitations.”<br/>•	GS-IV (Ethics): Justice, equality, representation as ethical values.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Way Forward<br/>1.	Early Implementation: Consider provisional application before delimitation.<br/>2.	Sub-Quota Debate: Address inclusivity within women’s reservation.<br/>3.	Party-Level Reforms: Encourage parties to nominate more women candidates beyond quotas.<br/>4.	Capacity Building: Leadership training, funding support, and mentorship for women politicians.<br/>5.	Local Linkages: Strengthen pipeline from Panchayati Raj (already 1.4 million women leaders) to higher legislatures.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Conclusion</div>
  <h3>Conclusion</h3><p>The Women’s Reservation Bill is a historic step for Indian democracy, but its delayed implementation risks diluting impact. True empowerment will require not only reserved seats but also dismantling patriarchal barriers within political parties and governance. For UPSC aspirants, this reform exemplifies the interplay of constitutional change, gender justice, and democratic deepening.</p>
